// Class 12 Geography practice bank, part 1: Fundamentals of Human Geography, chapters 1-6.
StudyData.addPractice(12, "geography", {
  "human-geography-nature-and-scope": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Geographers call road and rail networks the 'arteries of circulation'. This is an example of:", options: ["a metaphor from the human body", "a determinist argument", "a quantitative model", "a regional classification"], answer: 0, explain: "Human geography uses body metaphors for both nature (eye of a storm, mouth of a river) and human networks (arteries of circulation), showing how closely the two are intertwined.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The stage in which humans were dominated by nature and worshipped it is described as the:", options: ["naturalisation of humans", "humanisation of nature", "spatial organisation", "areal differentiation"], answer: 0, explain: "In the early stage of low technology, humans adapted to nature. This environmental determinism is called the naturalisation of humans.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Human geography as the study of 'the changing relationship between the unresting man and the unstable earth' is the view of:", options: ["Ratzel", "Ellen C. Semple", "Vidal de la Blache", "Griffith Taylor"], answer: 1, explain: "This definition is by Ellen C. Semple. Ratzel spoke of the synthetic study of human societies and the earth's surface.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The 'quantitative revolution' in human geography took place during:", options: ["the early colonial period", "the 1930s inter-war period", "the late 1950s to late 1960s", "the 1990s"], answer: 2, explain: "Computers and statistical tools were used to map human phenomena in the late 1950s to late 1960s, the phase of spatial organisation.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Medical geography is linked to which sister discipline?", options: ["Psephology", "Demography", "Anthropology", "Epidemiology"], answer: 3, explain: "Medical geography, a sub-field of social geography, links with epidemiology, the study of disease patterns.", level: 1 },

      { q: "Which statement about possibilism is correct?", options: ["Nature controls every human activity.", "Humans can create possibilities from what nature offers, using technology.", "Humans must stop all development to protect nature.", "It emerged only with the quantitative revolution."], answer: 1, explain: "Possibilism holds that nature offers opportunities and humans, with better technology, create possibilities. This is the humanisation of nature.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A geographer studies why housing, health care and education differ among social groups in a city, aiming to improve social well-being. Which school does this reflect?", options: ["Radical school", "Behavioural school", "Welfare (humanistic) school", "Areal differentiation"], answer: 2, explain: "The welfare or humanistic school is concerned with the social well-being of people: housing, health and education.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Neo-determinism is called 'stop and go determinism'.\nReason (R): It says humans may go ahead with development when nature permits but must slow down or stop when its limits are crossed, like a driver at traffic lights.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The traffic-light comparison is exactly why Griffith Taylor's middle path got the name 'stop and go'.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Benda, a tribal boy of the Abujh Maad forests, gets food, shelter and medicine directly from the forest. His way of life is closest to:", options: ["possibilism", "neo-determinism", "environmental determinism", "post-modernism"], answer: 2, explain: "Direct dependence on nature with low technology is the stage of environmental determinism (naturalisation of humans).", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which pairing of sub-field and sister discipline is wrong?", options: ["Cultural geography – anthropology", "Medical geography – epidemiology", "Electoral geography – demography", "Behavioural geography – psychology"], answer: 2, explain: "Electoral geography links with psephology, the study of elections. Demography is the sister discipline of population geography.", level: 2 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The quantitative revolution made human geography more humane.\nReason (R): It used computers and statistical tools to map human phenomena.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "R is true. The quantitative approach was criticised as dehumanised, and this discontent produced the humanistic, radical and behavioural schools in the 1970s. So A is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Arrange these approaches in the order in which they appeared:\n(a) areal differentiation  (b) exploration and description  (c) spatial organisation  (d) regional analysis", options: ["(b), (a), (d), (c)", "(b), (d), (a), (c)", "(d), (b), (a), (c)", "(b), (d), (c), (a)"], answer: 1, explain: "Exploration and description (early colonial), regional analysis (later colonial), areal differentiation (1930s), spatial organisation (late 1950s to 1960s).", level: 3 },
      { q: "A city lets factories expand freely until its river becomes badly polluted, then halts new expansion until treatment plants are built, and resumes growth afterwards. This policy best illustrates:", options: ["environmental determinism", "neo-determinism", "regional analysis", "the radical school"], answer: 1, explain: "Going ahead when nature permits and stopping when limits are crossed is Griffith Taylor's 'stop and go' neo-determinism.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which statement correctly contrasts the 1990s post-modernism with the late-1950s spatial organisation approach?", options: ["Both sought universal laws to explain human phenomena.", "Post-modernism questioned grand generalisations and stressed local context, while spatial organisation sought general laws using statistics.", "Spatial organisation stressed lived experience, while post-modernism relied on computers.", "Post-modernism was the first approach to describe new colonial areas."], answer: 1, explain: "Spatial organisation used statistics and physical laws to generalise. Post-modernism rejected grand generalisations and focused on each local context.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define human geography.", a: "Human geography studies the inter-relationship between the physical environment and the socio-cultural environment created by human beings through mutual interaction.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Who proposed neo-determinism, and by what other name is it known?", a: "Griffith Taylor proposed it. It is also called 'stop and go determinism'.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between the welfare (humanistic) school and the radical school of human geography.", a: "1. The welfare or humanistic school is concerned with the social well-being of people: housing, health and education.\n2. The radical school used Marxian theory to explain the basic cause of poverty, deprivation and social inequality, linking them to the development of capitalism.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why are roads and railways called 'arteries of circulation'? Give two body metaphors used for nature.", a: "1. Like arteries carry blood, road and rail networks carry people and goods, showing the close link between humans and their environment.\n2. Metaphors for nature: eye of a storm, mouth of a river (also snout of a glacier, neck of an isthmus).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain environmental determinism with the example of Benda.", a: "1. In early stages, with low technology, humans were dominated by nature; they listened to it, feared its fury and worshipped it. Nature was 'Mother Nature'. This is environmental determinism.\n2. Benda, a tribal boy of the Abujh Maad forests of central India, depends directly on nature for food, shelter and medicine.\n3. This stage is the naturalisation of humans, in which people adapt to the dictates of nature.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Name three sub-fields of social geography with the sister discipline each links to.", a: "1. Behavioural geography – psychology.\n2. Cultural geography – anthropology.\n3. Medical geography – epidemiology (also: geography of social well-being – welfare economics; geography of leisure – sociology; historical geography – history).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain areal differentiation and spatial organisation as approaches in human geography.", a: "1. Areal differentiation (1930s, inter-war period) identified the uniqueness of a region and explained how and why it differs from other regions.\n2. Spatial organisation (late 1950s to late 1960s) used computers, statistical tools and laws of physics to map human phenomena; this was the quantitative revolution.\n3. The second was criticised as dehumanised, which led to the humanistic, radical and behavioural schools in the 1970s.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Trace the changing relationship between humans and nature through the stages of naturalisation of humans, humanisation of nature and neo-determinism.", a: "1. Naturalisation of humans: with low technology, humans were dominated by nature, adapted to it and worshipped it (environmental determinism); e.g. Benda in the Abujh Maad forests.\n2. Humanisation of nature: with better technology humans create possibilities from nature's resources (possibilism).\n3. Signs of human activity such as ports, health resorts, fields, settlements and industries appear everywhere; e.g. Trondheim in Norway, where technology allows comfortable life in harsh winters.\n4. Neo-determinism (Griffith Taylor) is a middle path, 'stop and go determinism': like a driver at traffic lights, humans go ahead when nature permits and stop when limits are crossed.\n5. Free run of development leads to problems such as global warming and resource depletion, which is why this balance is needed.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Describe the humanistic, radical and behavioural schools and post-modernism that appeared after the quantitative revolution.", a: "1. They arose in the 1970s from discontent with the dehumanised quantitative approach.\n2. Humanistic (welfare) school: concerned with social well-being such as housing, health and education.\n3. Radical school: used Marxian theory to explain poverty, deprivation and inequality, linking them to capitalism.\n4. Behavioural school: stressed lived experience and the perception of space by social categories based on ethnicity, race, religion, etc.\n5. Post-modernism (1990s): questioned grand generalisations and universal theories and stressed the importance of each local context.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A hill town builds ski resorts and heated homes with modern technology, so people live comfortably though winters are harsh. Elsewhere, a forest community still gets food, shelter and medicine directly from the forest. Planners now say that development should pause whenever the town's lake shows signs of pollution, and restart once it is cleaned.\n(i) Which approach does the hill town illustrate?\n(ii) Which approach does the forest community illustrate?\n(iii) Name the approach in the planners' view and its proposer.", a: "(i) Possibilism (humanisation of nature): technology creates possibilities from what nature offers.\n(ii) Environmental determinism (naturalisation of humans): people adapt to nature and depend on it directly.\n(iii) Neo-determinism or 'stop and go determinism', proposed by Griffith Taylor: go ahead when nature permits and stop when limits are crossed.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
,
  "world-population": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Density of population is calculated as:", options: ["area ÷ number of people", "births − deaths", "number of people ÷ area", "number of people × area"], answer: 2, explain: "Density is the ratio of the number of people to the size of land, usually in persons per sq km.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Of the 10 most populous countries of the world, how many are in Asia?", options: ["3", "4", "5", "6"], answer: 3, explain: "The 10 most populous countries hold about 60 per cent of the world's population, and 6 of them are in Asia.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Katanga–Zambia copper belt is densely populated mainly because of:", options: ["mineral deposits", "fertile loamy soil", "religious importance", "a moderate climate"], answer: 0, explain: "Minerals attract industries and workers, so mining regions such as the Katanga–Zambia copper belt support large populations.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Crude Birth Rate is the number of:", options: ["live births in a year per thousand of population", "deaths in a year per thousand of population", "live births per hundred women", "people migrating in a year per thousand"], answer: 0, explain: "CBR = (live births in a year ÷ mid-year population) × 1000.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these areas is sparsely populated?", options: ["Ganga plains", "North-western Europe", "Hot and cold deserts", "Kobe–Osaka region"], answer: 2, explain: "Areas near the poles, hot and cold deserts, high mountains and high-rainfall equatorial zones are sparsely populated.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A region has an area of 2,500 sq km and a population of 10,00,000. Its density is:", options: ["250 persons per sq km", "400 persons per sq km", "2,500 persons per sq km", "4,000 persons per sq km"], answer: 1, explain: "Density = 1,000,000 ÷ 2,500 = 400 persons per sq km.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): River valleys are among the most densely populated areas of the world.\nReason (R): Fresh water is easily available there and the flat land suits farming and settlement.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Water for drinking, irrigation and transport, along with level land, draws people to river valleys.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "In a town, in one year there were 3,000 births, 1,200 deaths, 800 in-migrants and 300 out-migrants. The actual growth of population was:", options: ["1,800", "2,600", "3,500", "2,300"], answer: 3, explain: "Actual growth = births − deaths + in-migration − out-migration = 3,000 − 1,200 + 800 − 300 = 2,300.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A village with a mid-year population of 1,500 recorded 45 live births in the year. Its Crude Birth Rate is:", options: ["3 per thousand", "15 per thousand", "30 per thousand", "45 per thousand"], answer: 2, explain: "CBR = (45 ÷ 1,500) × 1000 = 30 per thousand.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A region's population falls between two censuses. Which situation could explain this?", options: ["Births exceed deaths and migration is nil.", "Deaths plus out-migration exceed births plus in-migration.", "In-migration exceeds out-migration.", "Births equal deaths and in-migration equals out-migration."], answer: 1, explain: "A fall means negative actual growth: deaths + out-migration > births + in-migration.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Country P had 40 million people in 2010 and 46 million in 2020, living on an unchanged area of 2,00,000 sq km. By how many persons per sq km did its density rise?", options: ["6", "15", "30", "46"], answer: 2, explain: "Density in 2010 = 40,000,000 ÷ 200,000 = 200. In 2020 = 46,000,000 ÷ 200,000 = 230. Rise = 30 persons per sq km.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): In stage 1 of the demographic transition, population grows slowly.\nReason (R): In stage 1 birth rates are low while death rates are high.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "Growth is slow in stage 1, but because both fertility and mortality are high, which offset each other. So R is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A town has a CBR of 20, a CDR of 8 and no migration. If its mid-year population is 50,000, how many people does it add in a year by natural growth?", options: ["400", "600", "1,000", "1,400"], answer: 1, explain: "Natural growth rate = 20 − 8 = 12 per thousand. Addition = 12 × 50,000 ÷ 1,000 = 600.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A country has a CBR of 38 and a CDR of 12, while its death rate is still falling as health care and sanitation improve. It is most likely in:", options: ["stage 1, with slow growth", "stage 2, with rapid growth", "stage 3, with stable population", "stage 3, with rapid growth"], answer: 1, explain: "High fertility with falling mortality is stage 2, producing a large gap between births and deaths and a large increase in population.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Define natural growth of population.", a: "Natural growth is the population change found by subtracting deaths from births in a region between two points of time (births − deaths).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the three components of population change.", a: "Births, deaths and migration.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why are hot deserts and high mountains sparsely populated? Give two reasons.", a: "1. Extreme climates (very hot or cold) discourage settlement; water is scarce in hot deserts.\n2. Rugged terrain and steep slopes make farming, roads and industry difficult and costly, so few people settle.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A district has a population of 2,40,000 and an area of 600 sq km. Write the formula and calculate its density.", a: "Density of population = number of people ÷ area.\n= 240,000 ÷ 600 = 400 persons per sq km.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain any three economic factors that affect the distribution of population, with examples.", a: "1. Minerals: they attract industries and workers, e.g. the Katanga–Zambia copper belt.\n2. Urbanisation: cities offer jobs, education, health care and better transport, so people move to them.\n3. Industrialisation: industrial belts attract large numbers of workers, e.g. the Kobe–Osaka region of Japan.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Distinguish between natural growth and actual growth of population, using the figures: births 5,000; deaths 2,000; in-migrants 1,500; out-migrants 500.", a: "1. Natural growth = births − deaths = 5,000 − 2,000 = 3,000.\n2. Actual growth = births − deaths + in-migration − out-migration = 3,000 + 1,500 − 500 = 4,000.\n3. Natural growth ignores migration, whereas actual growth includes net migration.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "State Malthus's theory and explain any two measures of population control.", a: "1. Thomas Malthus (1798) stated that the number of people would increase faster than the food supply, leading to famine, disease and war.\n2. Family planning (spacing or preventing births), with access to services, is a key measure.\n3. Awareness, free contraceptives and tax disincentives for large families (active population policies) also help.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the pattern of distribution of the world's population. Name densely and sparsely populated regions and give reasons for the unevenness.", a: "1. The distribution is very uneven: about 90 per cent of people live in about 10 per cent of the land area.\n2. The 10 most populous countries hold about 60 per cent of the population; 6 of them are in Asia.\n3. Densely populated: north-eastern USA, north-western Europe, and South, South-East and East Asia.\n4. Sparsely populated: areas near the poles, hot and cold deserts, high mountains and high-rainfall equatorial zones.\n5. Reasons: geographical (water, plains, climate, soils), economic (minerals, urbanisation, industry) and social-cultural factors (religious places, political unrest).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain how demographic transition links population change with the change of a society from rural, agrarian and illiterate to urban, industrial and literate. Why is stage 2 called a stage of rapid growth?", a: "1. The theory describes how fertility and mortality change as society develops.\n2. Stage 1: high fertility and high mortality; slow growth; epidemics, variable food supply, low life expectancy; people are illiterate and mostly in agriculture.\n3. Stage 2: fertility stays high at first, then declines, while mortality falls with better sanitation and health care.\n4. Because births far exceed deaths in stage 2, there is a large increase in population.\n5. Stage 3: both fertility and mortality decline considerably; people are urban and literate and deliberately limit family size, so population is stable or grows slowly.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Country Z had a mid-year population of 20,00,000 and an area of 5,000 sq km. In the following year it recorded 60,000 births, 20,000 deaths, 8,000 in-migrants and 4,000 out-migrants.\n(i) Calculate its CBR and CDR.\n(ii) Calculate natural growth and actual growth.\n(iii) Find the population density at the end of the year (to the nearest whole number) and say which demographic stage it probably resembles.", a: "(i) CBR = (60,000 ÷ 2,000,000) × 1000 = 30 per thousand. CDR = (20,000 ÷ 2,000,000) × 1000 = 10 per thousand.\n(ii) Natural growth = 60,000 − 20,000 = 40,000. Actual growth = 40,000 + 8,000 − 4,000 = 44,000.\n(iii) Population at year end = 2,000,000 + 44,000 = 2,044,000. Density = 2,044,000 ÷ 5,000 = 408.8, about 409 persons per sq km. With high CBR and a much lower CDR it resembles stage 2 of the demographic transition (rapid growth).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
,
  "human-development": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The Human Development Index gives each country a score that lies:", options: ["between 0 and 100", "between 1 and 10", "between 0 and 1", "between −1 and +1"], answer: 2, explain: "The HDI score lies between 0 and 1; the closer to 1, the higher the human development.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The concept of human development was introduced by:", options: ["Amartya Sen", "Mahbub-ul-Haq", "Thomas Malthus", "Griffith Taylor"], answer: 1, explain: "Dr Mahbub-ul-Haq introduced the concept; Prof. Amartya Sen added the capability approach. The UNDP has published the Human Development Report since 1990.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is NOT one of the four pillars of human development?", options: ["Equity", "Sustainability", "Productivity", "Profitability"], answer: 3, explain: "The four pillars are equity, sustainability, productivity and empowerment.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which set lists the six basic needs of the ILO's basic needs approach?", options: ["Health, education, food, water supply, sanitation, housing", "Health, education, income, employment, transport, housing", "Food, clothing, income, water supply, sanitation, energy", "Education, health, freedom, food, housing, communication"], answer: 0, explain: "The ILO's six basic needs are health, education, food, water supply, sanitation and housing.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In the HDI, the health dimension is measured by:", options: ["number of hospitals", "life expectancy at birth", "infant death rate only", "per capita food supply"], answer: 1, explain: "Health is measured by life expectancy at birth; education by literacy and schooling; access to resources by purchasing power in US dollars.", level: 1 },

      { q: "In which approach to human development are people treated as passive beneficiaries or targets of development?", options: ["Income approach", "Welfare approach", "Capability approach", "Basic needs approach"], answer: 1, explain: "The welfare approach sees people as recipients of government spending on education, health and amenities, not as active participants.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Growth and development are not the same thing.\nReason (R): Growth is quantitative and value neutral, while development is a qualitative change that is always value positive.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Growth can be positive or negative, but development needs a positive change in quality, so the two differ.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A city's population doubles in ten years, but slums spread and water supply and sanitation do not improve. This shows:", options: ["development without growth", "both growth and development", "a fall in size with no change in quality", "growth without development"], answer: 3, explain: "The city has grown in size, but without a positive change in quality of life there is no development.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A country's three dimension values are 0.6 (health), 0.9 (education) and 0.3 (access to resources). Adding them with equal weights of 1/3 each, as the NCERT describes, its HDI is:", options: ["0.3", "0.9", "0.6", "1.8"], answer: 2, explain: "HDI = (0.6 + 0.9 + 0.3) ÷ 3 = 1.8 ÷ 3 = 0.6.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A country has high national income, but girls and members of socially backward groups are denied access to schooling. Which pillar of human development is mainly lacking?", options: ["Equity", "Sustainability", "Productivity", "Empowerment"], answer: 0, explain: "Equity means equal access to opportunities for everybody irrespective of gender, race, income or caste.", level: 2 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Sustainability requires that opportunities remain available for every generation.\nReason (R): Each generation has the right to use resources freely without regard to the future.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true. R is false because sustainability means resources must be used so that future generations are not deprived.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Country P has a higher per capita income than Country Q, but lower life expectancy and weaker schooling. Which conclusion fits the idea of human development?", options: ["P must rank higher in HDI because income decides development.", "Q may rank higher in HDI despite a lower income.", "The two must have equal HDI.", "HDI cannot be compared across countries."], answer: 1, explain: "HDI gives each of health, education and access to resources a weight of 1/3, so income alone does not decide the rank.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which pairing of thinker and idea is correct?", options: ["Mahbub-ul-Haq – development as enlarging people's choices", "Amartya Sen – ranking countries by income alone", "ILO – capability approach", "UNDP – Gross National Happiness"], answer: 0, explain: "Haq defined human development as enlarging choices. Sen's capability approach stresses freedom and capabilities, the ILO proposed the basic needs approach, and GNH is Bhutan's measure.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A state greatly raises school enrolment and life expectancy while the average purchasing power stays the same. Using equal weights for the three dimensions, its HDI will:", options: ["fall, as income did not rise", "remain unchanged", "rise, since two of the three equally weighted dimensions improved", "rise only if income also rises"], answer: 2, explain: "Each dimension carries a weight of 1/3 and the HDI is the sum of the weighted values; two rising values with one constant raise the total.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Who introduced the concept of human development, and which organisation has published the Human Development Report every year since 1990?", a: "Dr Mahbub-ul-Haq introduced the concept; the UNDP (United Nations Development Programme) has published the report since 1990.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is Gross National Happiness?", a: "It is Bhutan's official measure of progress, which values well-being and the environment above material growth alone.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between the welfare approach and the capability approach to human development.", a: "1. Welfare approach: people are beneficiaries or targets of development, and the focus is on higher government spending on education, health and amenities; people are passive recipients.\n2. Capability approach (Prof. Amartya Sen): building people's capabilities in health, education and access to resources is the key to development, so people are active participants.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Name the three dimensions of the Human Development Index and the indicator used for each.", a: "1. Health: life expectancy at birth.\n2. Education: literacy and schooling (enrolment) indicators.\n3. Access to resources: purchasing power in US dollars (each dimension has a weight of 1/3).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain the pillars of equity and sustainability with Indian or general examples.", a: "1. Equity means equal access to opportunities for everybody irrespective of gender, race, income or caste. In India, women and socially backward groups often lack access to schooling.\n2. Sustainability means continuity in the availability of opportunities for every generation.\n3. Resources must be used so that future generations are not deprived; wasting environmental, financial or human resources violates it.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why is income alone not an adequate measure of human development? Give three arguments.", a: "1. Human development is about enlarging people's choices and their quality of life, not just earning money.\n2. It has three key areas: a long and healthy life, knowledge, and enough means for a decent life; income covers only the third.\n3. The size or income of a country alone does not decide its human development; some smaller or less rich countries do better than larger, richer ones.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What is the Human Poverty Index? How does it differ from the HDI?", a: "1. The Human Poverty Index measures the shortfall in human development.\n2. Indicators: probability of not surviving to age 40, adult illiteracy, lack of clean water and underweight children.\n3. The HDI instead measures achievement in health, education and access to resources on a 0 to 1 scale.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain how the Human Development Index is measured. How are countries grouped by it?", a: "1. The HDI ranks countries on a score between 0 and 1.\n2. It uses three dimensions: health, education and access to resources.\n3. Health = life expectancy at birth; education = literacy and schooling indicators; access to resources = purchasing power in US dollars.\n4. Each dimension has a weightage of 1/3, and the HDI is the sum of these weighted values; the closer to 1, the higher the human development.\n5. Countries are grouped into very high, high, medium and low human development.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Compare the characteristics of countries with high and low human development.", a: "1. High-HDI countries invest more in the social sector, especially education and health.\n2. They are generally free from political turmoil and have stable societies.\n3. Low-HDI countries often face political turmoil, social instability, civil war and famine.\n4. They have a high incidence of disease and poor access to schooling and health care.\n5. Size or income alone does not decide the level; some small or less rich countries do better than larger, richer ones.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Country X spends heavily on schools and health care, is politically stable and ensures that girls and boys have equal access to schooling. Country Y has suffered civil war and famine for years, and its people have little access to clean water and health services.\n(i) Which country is likely to have a higher HDI?\n(ii) Give two reasons.\n(iii) Name the three dimensions on which this index is based.", a: "(i) Country X.\n(ii) It invests in the social sector (education and health), and it is free from turmoil and offers equal access to opportunity (equity). Country Y's war and famine damage health, education and access to resources.\n(iii) Health, education and access to resources.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
,
  "primary-activities": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Workers engaged in primary activities are called:", options: ["blue-collar workers", "white-collar workers", "gold-collar workers", "red-collar workers"], answer: 3, explain: "Primary workers (hunters, herders, farmers, miners) are red-collar workers; secondary are blue-collar, quaternary white-collar and quinary gold-collar.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Shifting cultivation is known as 'jhuming' in:", options: ["north-eastern India", "Central America and Mexico", "Indonesia and Malaysia", "the Canadian prairies"], answer: 0, explain: "It is jhuming in north-eastern India, milpa in Central America and Mexico, and ladang in Indonesia and Malaysia.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A collective farm of the erstwhile Soviet Union was called:", options: ["Ranch", "Kolkhoz", "Plantation", "Ladang"], answer: 1, explain: "The kolkhoz was based on social ownership of the means of production and collective labour.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The temperate grasslands of Argentina used for extensive commercial grain cultivation are called:", options: ["steppes", "prairies", "velds", "pampas"], answer: 3, explain: "The steppes are in Eurasia, prairies in Canada and the USA, pampas in Argentina and velds in South Africa.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which country specialises in flower growing, especially tulips, under market gardening and horticulture?", options: ["Denmark", "New Zealand", "Argentina", "The Netherlands"], answer: 3, explain: "The Netherlands is the major supplier of flowers, especially tulips, to urban markets of Europe.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The cultivation of grapes, producing fine wines, is a speciality of:", options: ["plantation agriculture", "extensive grain cultivation", "Mediterranean agriculture", "truck farming"], answer: 2, explain: "Viticulture is a speciality of Mediterranean agriculture, which also supplies citrus fruits.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A mining company finds a coal seam close to the surface and wants large, rapid output at low cost with few safety risks. It should use:", options: ["underground (shaft) mining", "shifting cultivation", "plantation methods", "surface (open-cast) mining"], answer: 3, explain: "Surface mining is the easiest and cheapest method for minerals near the surface, with low safety and equipment costs.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): In truck farming, the distance of farms from the market is decided by how far a truck can travel overnight.\nReason (R): Truck farming specialises in growing vegetables for distant urban markets.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "Both are true, but the overnight limit of a truck is the reason for the distance, not the fact that vegetables are grown; the reason given does not explain the assertion.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Farmers of a Danish village voluntarily pool their resources to buy machinery and sell their produce together. This is an example of:", options: ["co-operative farming", "collective farming", "plantation agriculture", "primitive subsistence farming"], answer: 0, explain: "Co-operative farming, where farmers voluntarily pool resources, has been very successful in Denmark.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which of these is an economic factor, rather than a physical factor, affecting mining?", options: ["Grade of the ore", "Size of the deposit", "Transport cost", "Mode of occurrence of the deposit"], answer: 2, explain: "Physical factors are size, grade and mode of occurrence. Economic factors are demand, technology, capital, labour and transport costs.", level: 2 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Surface mining is generally cheaper than underground mining.\nReason (R): In surface mining, workers face poisonous gases, fires, floods and the risk of caving in.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true. R describes the risks of underground mining, so it is false for surface mining.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Which region is correctly matched with its type of farming?", options: ["Denmark – shifting cultivation", "Canterbury plains of New Zealand – extensive commercial grain cultivation", "Netherlands – collective farming", "Malaysia – dairy farming"], answer: 1, explain: "The Canterbury plains are among the extensive grain regions (with steppes, prairies, pampas, velds and downs). Denmark is known for co-operative farming and Malaysia for plantation rubber.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which pair of features belongs to intensive subsistence agriculture in monsoon Asia?", options: ["Small farms, high labour use and farmyard manure", "Very large mechanised farms and low labour use", "Single-crop estates and heavy capital", "Slash and burn and abandonment of land"], answer: 0, explain: "Intensive subsistence agriculture has small farms, is labour intensive and uses farmyard manure. The other options describe commercial grain, plantation and shifting cultivation.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which feature does NOT match plantation agriculture?", options: ["Large estates with heavy capital", "Single-crop specialisation", "Equal emphasis on crops and animal husbandry", "Good transport and cheap labour"], answer: 2, explain: "Equal emphasis on crops and animals is mixed farming. Plantations specialise in a single crop such as tea, rubber, coffee or cocoa.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Who are red-collar workers?", a: "Workers engaged in primary activities such as hunting, gathering, pastoralism, fishing, forestry, agriculture and mining are called red-collar workers.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the shifting cultivation practices of Central America and Mexico and of Indonesia and Malaysia.", a: "Milpa in Central America and Mexico; ladang in Indonesia and Malaysia.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State any two differences between nomadic herding and commercial livestock rearing.", a: "1. Nomadic herding is a subsistence activity in which herders move with animals in search of pasture and water; commercial livestock rearing is organised, on permanent ranches.\n2. Commercial rearing is capital intensive and specialises in one type of animal (as in New Zealand, Australia, Argentina, Uruguay, USA); nomads keep a variety (sheep, goats, camels, yaks, llamas, reindeer).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State two features of Mediterranean agriculture.", a: "1. It is highly specialised and supplies citrus fruits.\n2. Viticulture (grape cultivation) is a speciality, producing fine wines. It is found around the Mediterranean, California, central Chile, south-western South Africa and south-west Australia.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Describe extensive commercial grain cultivation.", a: "1. It is carried on mainly for wheat, on very large, fully mechanised farms.\n2. It is practised in semi-arid mid-latitude areas: Eurasian steppes, Canadian and American prairies, Argentine pampas, South African velds, Australian downs and the Canterbury plains of New Zealand.\n3. Output is for the market, with few workers because of the large scale of mechanisation.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Write a short note on dairy farming.", a: "1. Dairy farming is the most advanced and efficient type of rearing milch animals.\n2. It is capital and labour intensive and is located near urban and industrial centres, which provide the market for fresh milk and milk products.\n3. Main regions: north-western Europe, Canada, and south-eastern Australia, New Zealand and Tasmania.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Distinguish between market gardening, truck farming and factory farming.", a: "1. Market gardening and horticulture: high-value vegetables, fruits and flowers for urban markets; e.g. flowers (tulips) in the Netherlands.\n2. Truck farming: only vegetables; farms lie within the distance a truck can travel overnight to the market.\n3. Factory farming: stall-fed poultry and cattle reared in north-western Europe.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe primitive subsistence agriculture and intensive subsistence agriculture.", a: "1. Primitive (shifting) cultivation clears land by slash and burn; crops are grown until soil fertility falls, then the farmer moves to a new patch.\n2. It is called jhuming in north-eastern India, milpa in Central America and Mexico, and ladang in Indonesia and Malaysia.\n3. Intensive subsistence agriculture is found in the densely populated monsoon lands of Asia.\n4. Farms are small, labour intensive, and use farmyard manure.\n5. It is of two types: one dominated by wet paddy, and the other by crops such as wheat, soyabean, barley and sorghum (northern China, Manchuria, North Korea, northern Japan).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain the factors affecting mining and describe the two methods of mining.", a: "1. Physical factors: size, grade and mode of occurrence of the deposits.\n2. Economic factors: demand, technology and capital, labour and transport costs.\n3. Surface (open-cast) mining is for minerals near the surface; it is the easiest and cheapest, with low safety and equipment costs and large, rapid output.\n4. Underground (shaft) mining is for deep ores, using vertical shafts and galleries with lifts, drills, haulage and ventilation.\n5. It is risky because of poisonous gases, fires, floods and caving in. Developed countries are retreating from mining because of high labour costs, while many developing countries depend on mineral earnings.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A farm in a semi-arid mid-latitude region covers thousands of hectares. Wheat is the chief crop, ploughing and harvesting are done entirely by machines, and most produce is sold in distant markets.\n(i) Identify the type of agriculture.\n(ii) Name two regions of the world where it is practised.\n(iii) Give two features of this type of farming.", a: "(i) Extensive commercial grain cultivation.\n(ii) Any two: Eurasian steppes, Canadian or American prairies, Argentine pampas, South African velds, Australian downs, Canterbury plains of New Zealand.\n(iii) Very large farms; fully mechanised operations; wheat as the main crop; production for the market.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
,
  "secondary-activities": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The word 'manufacturing' literally means:", options: ["to make by machine", "to make in bulk", "to make by hand", "to make for export"], answer: 2, explain: "Manufacturing literally means 'to make by hand', though today it mostly means goods made by power-driven machines.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Workers in secondary activities are called:", options: ["red-collar workers", "white-collar workers", "gold-collar workers", "blue-collar workers"], answer: 3, explain: "Secondary workers are blue-collar workers; high-tech and quaternary workers are white-collar.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which industry is generally located near its source of raw material because the material is heavy and loses weight in processing?", options: ["Watch-making", "Sugar", "Software", "Printing"], answer: 1, explain: "Steel, sugar and cement use heavy, cheap, weight-losing raw materials, so they locate near the raw material.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The aluminium industry tends to locate near:", options: ["an assured supply of cheap power", "a large market", "a seaport only", "a tourist centre"], answer: 0, explain: "Industries using much power, such as aluminium, locate near energy supply.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Silicon Valley, a technopolis, is located near:", options: ["Seattle", "Tokyo", "Los Angeles", "San Francisco"], answer: 3, explain: "Silicon Valley is near San Francisco; the Silicon Forest is near Seattle.", level: 1 },

      { q: "Iron and steel is called a basic industry because:", options: ["it is owned by the government", "it is located near ports", "its products are used to make other goods", "it uses forest raw materials"], answer: 2, explain: "Basic industries make products used as inputs for other industries, e.g. iron and steel and machine tools.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A potter works at home with family members, using local clay and simple tools. This is a:", options: ["small-scale industry", "cottage industry", "large-scale industry", "high-tech industry"], answer: 1, explain: "A cottage (household) industry is the smallest unit, using local raw material and simple tools at home.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Sugar mills are usually located near sugarcane-growing areas.\nReason (R): Sugarcane is a heavy, weight-losing raw material, so transporting it over long distances is costly.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Industries using heavy, cheap, weight-losing raw materials locate close to them to save transport costs.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A firm assembles small electronic components from parts that can be obtained anywhere. It makes small quantities, employs few workers, causes little pollution and needs only good road access. It is a:", options: ["footloose industry", "cottage industry", "basic industry", "agro-based industry"], answer: 0, explain: "Footloose industries are not tied to a specific raw material, produce small quantities, are generally non-polluting and need accessibility by road.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A factory uses an assembly line, specialised workers, advanced technology and serves a large market. It is best classified by size as:", options: ["cottage industry", "small-scale industry", "large-scale industry", "household industry"], answer: 2, explain: "Large-scale industry needs large markets, varied raw materials, enormous energy, specialised workers, advanced technology and assembly-line mass production.", level: 2 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): High-tech industries employ mainly blue-collar workers.\nReason (R): High-tech industry is based on intensive research and development, and white-collar professionals form a large share of its workers.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "R is true. Because research is central, high-tech industry uses mainly white-collar professionals, so A is false.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Which pair of industry and classification is correct?", options: ["Cement – mineral-based, non-metallic", "Aluminium – ferrous", "Jute textiles – forest-based", "Leather – chemical-based"], answer: 0, explain: "Cement is a non-metallic mineral-based industry. Aluminium is non-ferrous, jute is agro-based and leather is animal-based.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A new auto-parts firm sets up near a big car assembly plant to benefit from its suppliers, services and skilled labour. This advantage is called:", options: ["economy of scale", "agglomeration economy", "footloose advantage", "market monopoly"], answer: 1, explain: "Agglomeration economies are the benefits that firms gain by locating close to a leader industry and other industries.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A metal industry needs 4 tonnes of ore to make 1 tonne of metal. If transport costs the same per tonne per km for ore and metal, moving the ore 200 km costs how many times as much as moving the metal 200 km, and where should the plant be placed?", options: ["Equal; anywhere", "4 times; near the market", "4 times; near the ore", "Half as much; near the ore"], answer: 2, explain: "Ore moves 4 tonnes × 200 km = 800 tonne-km against 200 tonne-km for the metal, so 4 times the cost. A weight-losing material pulls the plant to the ore source.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is the meaning of manufacturing?", a: "Manufacturing literally means 'to make by hand'. It is the transformation of raw materials into higher-value products, today mostly by power-driven machines.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is automation?", a: "Automation is manufacturing without the aid of human thinking during the process, using feedback and closed-loop computer control.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Differentiate mass production from the craft method.", a: "1. Mass production makes large quantities of standardised parts, each worker repeating one task.\n2. The craft method makes only a few pieces, usually to order.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Distinguish between cottage industry and small-scale industry.", a: "1. A cottage industry is the smallest unit, in which artisans work at home with family members, using local raw material and simple tools, e.g. pottery.\n2. A small-scale industry works outside the home, using local raw material, simple power-driven machines and semi-skilled labour; it is important for employment in China, India, Indonesia and Brazil.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Classify industries on the basis of raw material, giving one example of each.", a: "1. Agro-based: food processing, sugar, textiles (cotton, jute, silk), rubber.\n2. Mineral-based: iron and steel (ferrous), aluminium (non-ferrous), cement (non-metallic).\n3. Chemical-based: petrochemicals, plastics, pharmaceuticals; forest-based: paper, furniture, lac; animal-based: leather, wool.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain agglomeration economies and the role of government policy in the location of industries.", a: "1. Agglomeration economies are the benefits of being near a leader industry and other industries, such as services, suppliers and skilled labour.\n2. Governments set up industries in chosen areas, with policy aimed at balanced regional development.\n3. Hence industries may be located in backward regions even where other location factors are not ideal.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe three features of modern large-scale manufacturing.", a: "1. Specialisation of skills: each worker does a part of the job.\n2. Mechanisation and technological innovation: power-driven machines, assembly-line mass production.\n3. A complex organisational structure and uneven geographic distribution of industries over the world.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe large-scale industry and compare it with small-scale industry.", a: "1. Large-scale industry needs large markets, varied raw materials, enormous energy and specialised workers.\n2. It uses advanced technology and assembly-line mass production.\n3. Small-scale industry works outside the home with local raw material, simple power-driven machines and semi-skilled labour.\n4. Small-scale industry gives much employment, as in China, India, Indonesia and Brazil, while large-scale production is capital intensive.\n5. Large units are located in a few places, so distribution is uneven.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "What are high-tech industries? Describe their features and name two technopolis areas.", a: "1. High-tech industry is the latest generation of manufacturing, based on intensive research and development.\n2. White-collar professionals form a large share of workers.\n3. Examples include robotics, computer-aided design (CAD), electronic controls and new pharmaceuticals.\n4. Plants are low, modern, neatly spaced buildings rather than massive factories.\n5. Concentrated, self-sustained, highly specialised high-tech areas are technopolis, e.g. Silicon Valley (near San Francisco) and Silicon Forest (near Seattle).", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A company plans a cement plant. Limestone, its main raw material, is heavy, cheap and loses much weight in processing. The state government offers incentives to start industries in a backward district.\n(i) Why should the plant be near the limestone source?\n(ii) Classify the industry on the basis of raw material.\n(iii) Which location factor is represented by the government's incentives?", a: "(i) Limestone is a heavy, cheap, weight-losing raw material, so locating near it lowers transport cost.\n(ii) Mineral-based (non-metallic) industry.\n(iii) Government policy, which aims at balanced regional development.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
,
  "tertiary-and-quaternary-activities": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Which is the world's single largest tertiary activity in terms of total registered jobs?", options: ["Retail trade", "Transport", "Banking", "Tourism"], answer: 3, explain: "Tourism is travel for recreation rather than business and is the single largest tertiary activity in total registered jobs.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Professionals engaged in the highest-level decision making and policy making are called:", options: ["gold-collar workers", "white-collar workers", "blue-collar workers", "red-collar workers"], answer: 0, explain: "Quinary activities involve the highest level of decision makers, called gold-collar professionals.", level: 1 },
      { q: "When outsourced work is transferred to another country, it is called:", options: ["franchising", "offshoring", "agglomeration", "retailing"], answer: 1, explain: "Outsourcing means giving work to an outside agency; when it goes overseas it is offshoring.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Call centres and data processing are examples of:", options: ["KPO", "quinary activity", "BPO", "primary activity"], answer: 2, explain: "Business Process Outsourcing handles routine processes such as call centres and data entry. KPO involves highly skilled knowledge work.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is a low-order service?", options: ["Consultancy", "Physician's practice", "Accountancy", "Grocery shop"], answer: 3, explain: "Low-order services such as grocery shops and laundries are common and widespread. Accountants, consultants and physicians offer high-order services.", level: 1 },

      { q: "Weekly or bi-weekly markets held where regular markets are absent are known as:", options: ["periodic markets", "departmental stores", "chain stores", "wholesale markets"], answer: 0, explain: "Periodic markets are held at regular intervals in rural areas without regular markets.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The first large-scale innovation in retailing was the:", options: ["chain store", "departmental store", "vending machine", "consumer co-operative"], answer: 3, explain: "Consumer co-operatives were the first large-scale innovation in retailing, followed by departmental stores and chain stores.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Quaternary activities can be outsourced.\nReason (R): They are not tied to resources or markets.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Because knowledge-based work does not depend on raw materials or a particular market, it can be given to agencies in other places or countries.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Patients from several countries travel to a metropolitan hospital in India for surgery. This is an example of:", options: ["periodic marketing", "KPO", "medical tourism", "primary activity"], answer: 2, explain: "Medical tourism is travel to another country for treatment; India has emerged as a leading destination, with world-class hospitals in metropolitan cities.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A global firm hires an Indian company to carry out legal research and banking analysis using highly skilled workers. This is:", options: ["BPO", "KPO", "a periodic market", "a quinary activity"], answer: 1, explain: "Knowledge Processing Outsourcing involves highly skilled work such as research and development, legal and banking work and intellectual property research.", level: 2 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Quinary workers are called gold-collar professionals.\nReason (R): Quaternary workers are called white-collar professionals.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "Both statements are true, but the colour of one group's collar does not explain the colour of the other's.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Which match between worker colour and activity is correct?", options: ["Red – secondary, blue – primary", "Red – primary, blue – secondary, white – quaternary, gold – quinary", "White – tertiary, gold – quaternary", "Blue – quinary, white – primary"], answer: 1, explain: "Red-collar workers are primary; blue-collar secondary; white-collar quaternary; gold-collar quinary.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of the following is a quinary activity?", options: ["A call-centre agent", "A data-entry operator", "A school teacher", "A senior government official framing policy"], answer: 3, explain: "Quinary activities are the highest level of decision and policy making. Teachers are quaternary workers, and call-centre and data-entry work is routine BPO.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which statement about trade is correct?", options: ["Departmental stores are managed with heads of departments handling purchasing and sales.", "Wholesalers sell directly to final consumers and never give credit.", "Chain stores sell only to wholesalers.", "Retail trade is done only through fixed stores."], answer: 0, explain: "In departmental stores the heads of departments manage purchasing and sales. Wholesalers supply retailers, often extending credit; retail also includes non-store forms like peddling, mail order and the internet.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is medical tourism?", a: "Medical tourism is travel to another country mainly to receive medical treatment; India has emerged as a leading destination.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is a periodic market?", a: "A periodic market is a rural market held at regular intervals, such as weekly or bi-weekly, where regular markets are absent.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between low-order and high-order services.", a: "1. Low-order services (grocery shops, laundries) are common and widespread, serving everyday needs.\n2. High-order services (accountants, consultants, physicians) are fewer and more specialised, needing special skills.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Differentiate between wholesale and retail trading.", a: "1. Retail trading sells goods directly to consumers, mostly through fixed stores.\n2. Wholesale trading is done through intermediary merchants and supply houses, who supply retail and chain stores and often extend credit to them.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Name and explain the main forms of retail trading.", a: "1. Consumer co-operatives: the first large-scale innovation in retailing.\n2. Departmental stores: heads of departments manage purchasing and sales.\n3. Chain stores: buy merchandise most economically; non-store forms include street peddling, handcarts, mail order, telephone, internet and vending machines.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Write three features of quaternary activities.", a: "1. They centre on research, development and information: the collection, production and dissemination of knowledge.\n2. They are not tied to resources or markets and can be outsourced.\n3. Workers are white-collar professionals, e.g. IT specialists, research scientists, teachers and financial analysts.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What is the digital divide? Why does it matter?", a: "1. The digital divide is the uneven access to opportunities from information and communication technology.\n2. It exists between countries (developed and developing) and within countries (cities and rural areas).\n3. It matters because people without access miss opportunities in education, jobs and services based on ICT.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the types of tertiary activities and give examples of each.", a: "1. Tertiary activities provide services rather than goods and depend on skills, experience and knowledge more than on machinery.\n2. Trade and commerce: rural and urban marketing centres, retail and wholesale trading.\n3. Transport and communication: movement of goods, people and information.\n4. Services range from low-order (grocery shops, laundries) to high-order (accountants, consultants, physicians).\n5. Professional services include health care, engineering, law and management; many people also work in the informal sector, e.g. as domestic help.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain tourism as a tertiary activity: factors for its growth, attractions, and the importance of medical tourism in India.", a: "1. Tourism is travel for recreation rather than business and is the largest tertiary activity in total registered jobs.\n2. Factors: rising demand (higher living standards, more leisure time) and better transport.\n3. Attractions: climate (warm sunny beaches), landscape (mountains, lakes, coasts), history and art, and culture and economy (ethnic customs, homestays).\n4. Examples: heritage homes in Goa and in Madikeri and Coorg in Karnataka.\n5. Medical tourism: patients travel abroad for treatment, and India, with world-class hospitals in metropolitan cities, has emerged as a leading country.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A company in Europe gives its customer-care calls and billing data to an agency in another country to reduce costs. It also hires a firm there for business research and legal work done by highly skilled professionals. Meanwhile, rural schools in the company's country still lack internet access that city schools enjoy.\n(i) Name the practice of giving work to an outside agency.\n(ii) Identify the two kinds of outsourcing described.\n(iii) What kind of divide do the schools illustrate?", a: "(i) Outsourcing (when the agency is in another country it is offshoring).\n(ii) Customer-care calls and billing data are Business Process Outsourcing (BPO). Business research and legal work by highly skilled professionals are Knowledge Processing Outsourcing (KPO).\n(iii) A digital divide within a country (rural versus urban access to ICT).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
